45.	 Pulmonary Embolism
Pulmonary Embolism (PE) results from thrombi, often from the deep 
veins of the lower limbs or pelvis, which are transported via the right heart 
into the pulmonary vasculature. Large emboli may cause obstruction to 
blood flow and result in life-threatening hypoxia and high mortality. PE 
should therefore be managed as a medical emergency. 
The risk factors and management for PE are similar to those for DVT. 
(See section on ‘DVT’). 
Causes 
(See section on ‘DVT’)
