Box 7-7:  Notes on anti-hypertensive medicines
Any of the five classes of major antihypertensive drugs can be used as first-line 
treatment. These are:
yy
Thiazide Diuretics 
yy
Calcium Channel Blockers 
yy
Angiotensin Converting Enzyme Inhibitors
yy
Angiotensin Receptor Blockers 
yy
Beta-blockers
In the general black population thiazide diuretics or calcium channel blockers, 
either as monotherapy or in some combination therapy, is preferrable. Angio­
tensin converting enzyme Inhibitors are not recommended as first-line drugs for 
uncomplicated hypertension in black patients.
Dual therapy should be started earlier when the blood pressure exceeds 
180/110 mmHg.
Additional anti-hypertensive drugs should be used if target blood pressure 
levels are not achieved. Add-on drugs should be chosen from first-line choices 
bearing in mind compelling indications and contraindications. 
Compelling indications for the choice of antihypertensives
yy
Left ventricular hypertrophy: ACE-I or ARB, CCB preferably Amlodipine 
yy
