60.	 Bronchial Asthma
Asthma is a chronic inflammatory disease of the bronchial airways, 
which manifests as recurrent episodes of wheeze, cough, chest tightness 
and shortness of breath, which is usually reversible with treatment. It 
is characterised by increased sensitivity to many environmental agents. 
Asthma is variable and may be associated with seasons like the rainy 
season or harmattan. Bronchial asthma occurs at all ages but peaks in 
childhood. It is classified as an allergic disease, and may be associated 
with a personal or family history of allergic rhinitis (hay fever), eczema or 
allergic conjunctivitis. 
Complications 
of 
asthma 
include 
pneumomediastinum, 
pneumothorax, subcutaneous emphysema and pneumonia. 
Causes 
yy
Allergens e.g. house dust mite, cockroach droppings, grass, pollen 
and animal hair
yy
Environmental factors e.g. air pollution, climatic changes, strong 
scents and smoke (including cigarette smoke and car fumes)
yy
Viral infections 
yy
Exercise
yy
Emotions and hyperventilation 
yy
Drugs e.g. aspirin, NSAIDS and beta-blockers such as propranolol
yy
Occupational exposure to industrial chemicals, dust and drug 
manufacturing
Symptoms
yy
Episodic breathlessness
yy
Tightness of the chest
yy
Cough - often nocturnal
yy
Wheeze
yy
Nocturnal symptoms. Any of the above symptoms waking up the 
patient at night
Signs
yy
Tachypnoea (fast breathing)
yy
Use of accessory muscles of respiration; neck and/or abdominal 
muscles
yy
Rhonchi/wheeze 

— Bronchial Asthma —
Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017
176
yy
