64.	 Lung Abscess
A lung abscess is defined as necrosis of the pulmonary parenchyma 
and formation of cavities containing necrotic tissue or purulent fluid, 
usually caused by microbial infection. Antibiotic management should be 
considered as soon as it is diagnosed, while awaiting confirmation of the 
causative organism and must be continued for 4-6 weeks.
Causes 
yy
Aspiration of infected secretions or tissue from the mouth and upper 
respiratory tract in the unconscious or semi-conscious patient e.g. in 
alcoholics, epileptics, anaesthetised or dental patients 
yy
Foreign body aspiration e.g. inhaled peanut, dentures, fish bone
yy
Inadequately treated bacterial pneumonia especially, gram 
negative bacteria like Klebsiella pneumoniae, and beta-haemolytic 
streptococci
yy
Staphylococcus aureus causing multiple lung abscesses
yy
Penetrative lung injury
yy
Partial obstruction of an airway by tumour or lymph node
yy
Septic emboli from other infected areas of the body e.g. right sided 
bacterial endocarditis
yy
Bronchiectasis
yy
Infected bullae in chronic lung disease
Symptoms
yy
Fever with swinging temperatures
yy
Cough, productive of copious amounts of purulent foul smelling 
sputum
