ities) yy Lifestyle (sedentary/active, homebound, light exposure, exercise) yy Travel (especially across time zones) yy Quality of life and exacerbation of co-morbid disorders',NULL,'yy FBC yy BUE and creatinine yy LFTs yy Blood glucose yy Thyroid function tests','Treatment objectives yy Reduction of waking symptoms yy Improvement of daytime function yy Reduction of distress yy Treatment of co-morbid conditions Non-pharmacological treatment yy Stimulus control therapy (avoid stimulating sleep environments such as leaving lights, TV and radio on) yy Relaxation training yy Cognitive Behavior Therapy yy Avoidance of day-time sleep yy Avoidance of excessive stimulant consumption pre-bed time (e.g.','Refer all cases for evaluation by psychologist or psychiatrist. 72. Insomnia Insomnia is defined as a subjective report of difficulty with sleep initiation, duration, consolidation, or quality that occurs despite adequate opportunity for sleep, and that results in some form of daytime impairment. Insomnia symptoms occur quite commonly in the general population. Risk factors for insomnia include increasing age, female sex, co-morbid disorders such as medical, psychiatric, substance use, and shift work. Patients with psychiatric and chronic pain disorders have relatively high rates of insomnia. Causes yy Behavioural - spending more time in bed in an effort to “catch up” on sleep yy Stress yy Learned habits which do not enhance sleep yy Cognitive distortions (e.g. if one does not sleep throughout the night, one has not slept at all) yy Medicines (e.g.for treatment of common cold, hypertension, asthma) yy Caffeine-containing beverages (e.g. coffee, tea) yy Withdrawal from alcohol and other drugs of abuse (e.g. cocaine, marijuana, amphetamines) yy Medical condition (e.g. sleep apnea, airway obstruction, liver disease, renal disease, thyroid disorders) yy Psychological and environmental factors yy Other psychiatric disorders such as anxiety and mood disorders yy Travel (especially across time zones leading to jet lag) yy Shift work','— Insomnia —
Standard Treatment Guidelines, 7th Edition, 2017
214
Referral Criteria 
Refer all cases for evaluation by psychologist or psychiatrist.
72.	 Insomnia
Insomnia is defined as a subjective report of difficulty with sleep 
initiation, duration, consolidation, or quality that occurs despite 
adequate opportunity for sleep, and that results in some form of daytime 
impairment. Insomnia symptoms occur quite commonly in the general 
population. Risk factors for insomnia include increasing age, female sex, 
co-morbid disorders such as medical, psychiatric, substance use, and shift 
work. Patients with psychiatric and chronic pain disorders have relatively 
high rates of insomnia. 
Causes 
yy
Behavioural - spending more time in bed in an effort to “catch up” 
on sleep
yy
Stress 
yy
Learned habits which do not enhance sleep
yy
Cognitive distortions (e.g. if one does not sleep throughout the night, 
one has not slept at all)
yy
Medicines (e.g.for treatment of common cold, hypertension, asthma)
yy
Caffeine-containing beverages (e.g. coffee, tea) 
yy
Withdrawal from alcohol and other drugs of abuse (e.g. cocaine, 
marijuana, amphetamines)
yy
Medical condition (e.g. sleep apnea, airway obstruction, liver 
disease, renal disease, thyroid disorders)
yy
Psychological and environmental factors 
yy
Other psychiatric disorders such as anxiety and mood disorders
yy
Travel (especially across time zones leading to jet lag) 
yy
Shift work
 
